Q: What can be considered as a best practice for manipulating values inside a vector in C++ A: <p>You might consider the red-headed stepchild of the standard library: <code>std::valarray</code>.</p>
<p><code>valarray</code> was originally designed for Cray-like vector processors, which were becoming obsolete around the time the C++ standard was originally released. And it remained kind of a curiosity for quite a while. But as fairly &quot;normal&quot; processors have added vector processing capabilities, compiler vendors have done some work on optimizing <code>std::valarray</code> to take advantage of availabale vector processing capabilities.</p>
<p>Here's a bit of code to compare doing the job with a <code>valarray</code> vs. a <code>vector</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include&lt;vector&gt;
#include&lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;valarray&gt;
#include &lt;chrono&gt;

// rewritten to just sum the vector, so we can more reasonably
// use it when dealing with larger arrays.
unsigned long long sumVector(auto const &amp;v) {
    unsigned long long ret = 0;
    for (std::size_t i=0; i&lt;v.size(); i++)
        ret += v[i];
    return ret;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
    using namespace std::chrono;

    int lower = atoi(argv[1]);
    int upper = atoi(argv[2]);

    std::valarray&lt;int&gt; v(upper-lower);

    for (int i = lower; i&lt;= upper; i++)
        v[i] = i;

    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v2{std::begin(v), std::end(v)};

    auto start = clock::now();
    auto result = v * v;
    auto sum = sumVector(result);
    auto stop = clock::now();   

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;vallaray Sum: &quot; &lt;&lt; sum;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\tTime: &quot; &lt;&lt; duration_cast&lt;nanoseconds&gt;(stop-start).count() &lt;&lt; &quot;ns\n&quot;;

    auto start2 = clock::now();
    for (auto &amp; v : v2) {
        v *= v;
    }
    auto sum2 = sumVector(v2);
    auto stop2 = clock::now();

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;vector   Sum: &quot; &lt;&lt; sum2;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\tTime: &quot; &lt;&lt; duration_cast&lt;nanoseconds&gt;(stop2-start2).count() &lt;&lt; &quot;ns\n&quot;;
}

</code></pre>
<p>I compiled this with: <code>g++ -O3 -march=native -std=c++23 mul.cpp</code>. Then, when I ran it with <code>0 10001</code> as arguments, I got:</p>
<pre><code>vallaray Sum: 333383335000  Time: 3079ns
vector   Sum: 333383335000  Time: 6876ns
</code></pre>
<p>It appears to me that <code>std::valarray</code> exposes something like an expression template. If we only time creation of the <code>result</code> object, it seems to take a nearly fixed time (of about 150 ns), regardless of the input size. So we only see the time to traverse the contents of the array when we actually read the results. To try to be at least somewhat fair, I've included the time to sum the array in both results. As shown above, this can still save a fair amount of time.</p>

Q: How to include h1, h2, and h3 all on a hero image without them stacking? A: <p><code>position: absolute</code> causes the behavior of stacking. If they have position: absolute, then you need to do further positioning and if you have multiple elements and need to position them <strong>relative</strong> to each-other, that could be difficult. Instead, you could remove the <code>position: absolute</code> rule and address the problem of gaps via <code>margin-bottom</code>.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.hero-image{
    background-image:linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, black), url(img-2.png);
    width: 100%;
    height: 70vh;
    background-size: cover;
    position: relative;
    border: 1 px solid black;
    background-attachment: fixed;

}

.hero-text{
    color: white;
    margin-left: 2%;
    flex-wrap: none;
    margin-bottom: -25px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="hero-image"&gt;
